  "query_preview": "..\n\nA high-pressure system over the northern Midwest is producing \nsoutheasterly surface winds across the northern Great Plains, \nresulting in moisture pooling in northeast Montana. The lack of \nsolar heating associated with the ongoing transition to winter is \nsignificantly limiting atmospheric mixing, resulting in an \ninversion over the region. The inversion, coupled with the \naforementioned moisture pool, is resulting in fog across northeast\nportions of the state.\n\nFog this morning-- like the past couple mornings-- is difficult to\nforecast exactly. However, Daniels, Sheridan, Roosevelt, and \nnortheast Valley Counties will likely see fog clear by mid-morning.\nFog near the Yellowstone valley is anticipated to clear by late \nmorning.\n\nFog aside, an upper-level ridge over Montana will result in \nwarmer than average temperatures through Tuesday.\n\nThe ridge will eventually exit eastward ahead of an upper-level\ntrough that will dig over Montana by Wednesday, resulting in\ntemperatures dropping significantly and returning to near \nseasonal averages.\n\nMoisture-ladden southwesterly flow aloft associated with the \ntrough will enable precipitation starting Wednesday. At this \ntime, the predominant mode is expected to be rain as opposed to \nsnow. Confidence will improve as time progresses.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Added fog in regions surrounding the Yellowstone Valley. No\n  further deviations from the NBM were made.\n\n- Confidence in fog placement and visibilities this morning is\n  moderate (60%).\n \n- Confidence in fair conditions persisting through Tuesday is \n  high (85%).\n\n- Confidence in mid-week trough timing is moderate to high (75%).\n\n- Confidence in precipitation mode and amounts next week is low\n  (20%).\n\n\nDON",
